{"id":"6e7d8b3e-34cb-4e45-a2f6-1c2187c5da82","object":"chat.completion","created":1784795115,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for the task \"DATA-SNAP-01\". The task description: \"Apply the registered sparse IDF scaling, row normalization, self-similarity removal, and degree construction without dense materialization.\" The bounded objective: \"SnapATAC2 spectral preprocessing through degree construction; eigensolving is excluded.\"\n\nFrom the fixture, each case has a `matrix` which is a list of lists of 0s and 1s (binary). The task is to compute IDF scaling, row normalization, self-similarity removal, and degree construction.\n\nLet's parse the instructions carefully:\n\n- Validate a nonempty rectangular matrix.\n- For each feature (column) count rows with a nonzero entry (document frequency). That's the number of rows where entry != 0.\n- Compute IDF as log(1 + number_of_rows / (1 + document_frequency)). Here _number_of_rows_ is total rows (N), document_frequency is count per feature (f). So IDF(f) = log(1 + N / (1 + f)).\n- Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree. But also row normalization and self-similarity removal.\n\nWait: \"row normalization, self-similarity removal\" - what does that entail? In SnapATAC2 preprocessing, typical steps: TF-IDF weighting, then L1 row normalization (or L2), then self-similarity removal (i.e., set diagonal to zero), then compute row sums (degree). However the description says: \"Apply the registered sparse IDF scaling, row normalization, self-similarity removal, and degree construction without dense materialization.\"\n\nThe output should contain: IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees.\n\nSpecifically: \"Round and return IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees.\"\n\nMoreover, \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: sparse_only, self_similarity_removed, finite_degree, numeric_tolerance.\"\n\nInterpretation: The matrix is sparse (binary), we should not densify. Self-similarity removed means that after normalization, we should set diagonal entries to zero (since self-similarity is row i vs row i). But note: the matrix is rows x columns (N x M). Self-similarity removal typically sets to zero entries where row index equals column index? That only makes sense if we are computing a similarity matrix, but here we are constructing a weighted matrix (IDF-scaled, normalized). Wait, maybe the matrix is a binary accessibility matrix (cells x peaks). After IDF scaling and row normalization, we construct a cell-by-cell similarity? No, the objective says \"degree construction\" - that sounds like constructing a graph where cells are nodes and edges are weighted by similarity. Then degree is sum of weights per node. Self-similarity removal would set the diagonal entries of the similarity matrix to zero.\n\nBut the case payload only gives a matrix (cells x features). The output is to be \"IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees\". The weighted matrix might be the cell-by-feature matrix after IDF scaling and row normalization, but then degree would be sum of weights per row? That would be row sum after normalization and self-similarity removal. However self-similarity removal on a cell-feature matrix doesn't make sense (there is no diagonal). So maybe \"self-similarity removal\" refers to setting to zero entries where the feature is the same as some self? No.\n\nLet's re-read: \"SnapATAC2 spectral preprocessing through degree construction\" - In SnapATAC2, the typical pipeline: (1) TF-IDF transform on binary accessibility matrix, (2) row L1 normalization, (3) compute self-similarity (i.e., dot product of row-normalized vectors) to get a cell-cell similarity matrix, (4) remove self-similarity (set diagonal to zero), (5) compute degree (row sums of similarity matrix). That yields degree vector for each cell. Then eigensolving on the graph Laplacian follows. So indeed the weighted matrix here is the cell-by-feature matrix after IDF and row normalization? Or the cell-by-cell similarity? The description says \"weighted matrix\" could be the similarity matrix. But the input is a matrix of cells x features, and we must compute IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees. It says \"Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree.\" That sounds like: after IDF scaling, for each row, sum the weighted entries to get degree? But then why row normalization? \"Apply ... row normalization\" before degree.\n\nLet's parse the bullet points:\n\n- For each feature count rows with a nonzero entry.\n- Compute IDF as log(1 + number_of_rows / (1 + document_frequency)).\n- Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree.\n\nThis suggests: IDF scaling -> then for each row, sum over columns (weighted entries) ->"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":958,"completion_tokens":1024,"total_tokens":1982,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1024},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":318},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}